_verdict no">=E2=9C=97 Incorrect</p><div><!-- DOMAIN: Design Infrastructure=
 Solutions -->
<p><b>Correct answer: Root certificate including public key</b></p>
<p>The root certificate's public key is what needs to be uploaded to the Az=
ure virtual network gateway for Point-to-Site (P2S) VPN connections. When a=
 client attempts to connect via P2S VPN, the gateway needs to verify that t=
he client certificate presented during authentication is trusted. It does t=
his by checking if the client certificate was issued by a Certificate Autho=
rity (CA) whose root certificate is trusted. By uploading the root certific=
ate (containing the public key) of your on-premises CA to the gateway, you =
are essentially telling the gateway to trust certificates issued by that CA=
. The private key of the root certificate remains securely on-premises and =
is never uploaded to Azure.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. Root certificate including private key</b> - The private key of th=
e root certificate should never be exposed or uploaded to any external syst=
em, including Azure. This would compromise the entire certificate infrastru=
cture. The root certificate's private key is used to sign other certificate=
s, and if compromised, an attacker could issue their own trusted certificat=
es.</p>
<p><b>C. Client certificate including private key</b> - While the client ce=
rtificate and its private key are necessary for the client device to authen=
ticate to the VPN, the gateway itself does not need the client certificate'=
s private key. The client's private key is used locally on the client devic=
e to prove its identity.</p>
<p><b>D. Client certificate including public key</b> - Uploading only the c=
lient certificate's public key to the gateway would not be sufficient for t=
he gateway to establish trust. The gateway needs to trust the CA that issue=
d the client certificate, which is achieved by uploading the root certifica=
te of the CA.</p>
<p><b>E. All of the above</b> - Only the root certificate's public key is r=
equired to be uploaded to the virtual network gateway. The other options ar=
e either security risks (uploading private keys) or unnecessary for establi=
shing trust.</p>
