<p><b>Correct answer: an auto-failover group</b></p>
<p>Auto-failover groups in Azure SQL Database are designed for transparent =
failover of a group of databases to a secondary region. This aligns perfect=
ly with the requirement for the payment processing system to remain availab=
le without administrative intervention in the event of a data center failur=
e. Auto-failover groups provide a single read-write listener endpoint that =
remains the same after failover, minimizing the impact on the middle-tier A=
PI. This solution also supports the required RPO (Recovery Point Objective)=
 and RTO (Recovery Time Objective) for business-critical applications. Sinc=
e the back-end tier is a SQL Server database, auto-failover groups are the =
most suitable disaster recovery solution.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. Always On Failover Cluster Instances</b> - Always On Failover Clus=
ter Instances (FCI) are primarily used for high availability within a singl=
e data center. While they provide redundancy, they do not address disaster =
recovery scenarios involving the failure of an entire region or data center=
. The case study specifies a requirement for the system to remain available=
 if a data center fails, making FCI an insufficient solution.</p>
<p><b>B. active geo-replication</b> - Active geo-replication creates readab=
le secondary databases in different regions. While it provides disaster rec=
overy capabilities, it requires application logic to handle the failover pr=
ocess, including changing connection strings to point to the secondary data=
base. The requirement states that failover must occur without any administr=
ative intervention and that the middle-tier should continue to operate with=
out additional configurations. Active geo-replication does not meet these r=
equirements as seamlessly as auto-failover groups.</p>
<p><b>C. Azure Site Recovery</b> - Azure Site Recovery (ASR) is a disaster =
recovery service that replicates virtual machines from a primary location t=
o a secondary location. While ASR can be used to protect the SQL Server vir=
tual machine, it involves a more complex failover process, including virtua=
l machine replication, failover orchestration, and potential DNS changes. T=
his adds administrative overhead and does not align with the requirement to=
 minimize management overhead and ensure automatic failover without adminis=
trative intervention. Furthermore, auto-failover groups are a more native a=
nd streamlined solution for SQL Database disaster recovery.</p>
