<p><b>Correct answer: Client certificate including private key</b></p>
<p>For Point-to-Site (P2S) VPN connections using native Azure certificate a=
uthentication, each client machine needs a client certificate installed. Th=
is certificate is used to authenticate the client to the Azure VPN gateway.=
 Crucially, the client certificate must include the private key, as this is=
 used to prove the client's identity during the VPN connection handshake. T=
he client certificate is derived from a root certificate that is trusted by=
 the Azure VPN gateway. The root certificate's public key is uploaded to Az=
ure, establishing the trust relationship. However, the client machines them=
selves need the client certificate with the private key for authentication.=
</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. Root certificate including private key</b> - The root certificate,=
 including its private key, should never be distributed to client machines.=
 The root certificate's private key is used to sign the client certificates=
 and should be kept securely on the certificate authority server. Distribut=
ing the root certificate's private key would compromise the entire certific=
ate infrastructure.</p>
<p><b>B. Root certificate including public key</b> - While the public key o=
f the root certificate is important (it's uploaded to Azure to establish tr=
ust), it's not what the client machines need to authenticate. The client ma=
chines require a client certificate that is issued by the root certificate =
authority and contains the client's private key.</p>
<p><b>D. Client certificate including public key</b> - The client certifica=
te's public key alone is insufficient for authentication. The VPN connectio=
n process requires the client to prove its identity using the private key a=
ssociated with the client certificate. Without the private key, the client =
cannot complete the authentication process.</p>
<p><b>E. All of the above</b> - This is incorrect because options A and D a=
re incorrect. The root certificate's private key should never be distribute=
d, and the client certificate requires the private key for authentication.<=
/p>
