<p><b>Correct answer: 180 Days</b></p>
<p>When using Azure Blob Storage with the Archive tier, it's crucial to und=
erstand the early deletion or early access costs. The Archive tier is desig=
ned for data that is rarely accessed and has a low storage cost but higher =
data retrieval costs. Microsoft imposes a minimum retention period. If a bl=
ob in the Archive tier is deleted or moved to a hotter tier (Cool or Hot) b=
efore 180 days, you'll be charged an early deletion fee or an early access =
fee, respectively. Therefore, to avoid these penalties, data should be stor=
ed for a minimum of 180 days in the Archive tier.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. 30 Days</b> - Storing data for only 30 days in the Archive tier wo=
uld result in significant early deletion or early access fees if the data i=
s then deleted or moved to a hotter tier. The minimum retention period is m=
uch longer.</p>
<p><b>B. 90 Days</b> - While 90 days is a longer retention period than 30 d=
ays, it's still significantly less than the required 180 days for the Archi=
ve tier. Early deletion or access fees would still apply.</p>
<p><b>C. 120 Days</b> - 120 days is closer to the minimum retention period,=
 but it's still not sufficient to avoid early deletion or early access fees=
. The minimum retention period for the Archive tier is 180 days.</p>
