tions -->
<p><b>Correct answer: Azure Internal Load Balancer</b></p>
<p>An Azure Internal Load Balancer is the correct choice because it allows =
you to distribute traffic to the VMs within the VMSS using a private IP add=
ress. Since the application should only be accessed via the Site-to-Site VP=
N from on-premises to Azure, using a private IP address is essential. The I=
nternal Load Balancer ensures that the VMs in the scale set are accessible =
only from within the Azure virtual network or from on-premises networks con=
nected via VPN or ExpressRoute. This meets the requirement of accessing the=
 scale set on a single private IP address and allows for SSH access through=
 the Network Security Group inbound rules.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. Azure Traffic Manager</b> - Azure Traffic Manager is a DNS-based t=
raffic load balancer. It distributes traffic to different endpoints based o=
n DNS resolution. It's primarily used for global traffic management and hig=
h availability across different regions, not for internal load balancing wi=
thin a virtual network. It doesn't provide a single private IP address for =
accessing the VMs in the scale set.</p>
<p><b>B. Azure Application Gateway</b> - Azure Application Gateway is a web=
 traffic load balancer that operates at Layer 7 of the OSI model. While it =
