<p><b>Correct answer: the Windows operating system and the Consumption plan=
 hosting plan</b></p>
<p>The Consumption plan in Azure Functions is designed specifically for sce=
narios where you want dynamic resource allocation and pay-per-execution bil=
ling. When you use the Consumption plan, Azure automatically scales the fun=
ction app based on the number of incoming events. You are only charged when=
 your functions are running. The Windows operating system is a supported pl=
atform for Azure Functions. This combination fulfills the requirements of d=
ynamic allocation and execution-based billing for the Event Grid triggered =
function app.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. the Windows operating system and the App Service plan hosting plan=
</b> - While Windows is a valid OS for Azure Functions, using a regular App=
 Service plan (even if it's a low-tier one) means you're paying for allocat=
ed resources regardless of whether your function is actively running. This =
contradicts the requirement of billing based on executions.</p>
<p><b>B. the Docker container and an App Service plan that uses the B1 pric=
ing tier</b> - Using a Docker container is a valid option for Azure Functio=
ns, offering flexibility in terms of runtime and dependencies. However, the=
 B1 pricing tier is part of the App Service plan, which means you're paying=
 for allocated resources even when the function isn't executing. This doesn=
't meet the requirement of billing based on executions. Also, while Docker =
containers are supported, they are not *required* for this scenario, and th=
e Consumption plan is a more direct solution.</p>
<p><b>D. the Docker container and an App Service plan that uses the S1 pric=
ing tier</b> - Similar to option B, using a Docker container with the S1 Ap=
p Service plan means you are paying for allocated resources, regardless of =
executions. The S1 tier provides dedicated resources, which is not necessar=
y when the requirement is to dynamically allocate resources and bill based =
on executions. The Consumption plan is the better choice for this requireme=
nt.</p>
