rastructure Solutions -->
<p><b>Correct answer: Virtual Machine Scale Set (VMSS)</b></p>
<p>A Virtual Machine Scale Set (VMSS) is the best solution for handling dra=
matic load variations and ensuring cost-effectiveness. VMSS allows you to a=
utomatically increase or decrease the number of VM instances based on deman=
d. You can configure autoscaling rules to add instances when the load incre=
ases (e.g., CPU usage exceeds a threshold) and remove instances when the lo=
ad decreases. This dynamic scaling ensures that you have enough resources t=
o handle peak loads without paying for idle resources during normal periods=
. This aligns perfectly with the requirement of handling 10x load spikes at=
 the end of the month and then scaling back down. VMSS also integrates well=
 with Azure Load Balancer for distributing traffic across the instances.</p=
>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. Availability set</b> - An availability set provides redundancy and=
 availability by distributing VMs across multiple fault domains and update =
domains within a datacenter. However, it does not automatically scale the n=
umber of VMs based on load. It's designed to protect against hardware failu=
res and planned maintenance, not to handle fluctuating workloads.</p>
<p><b>B. Internal load balancer</b> - An internal load balancer distributes=
 traffic across VMs within a virtual network. While it's a component often =
used with scalable applications, it doesn't provide the scaling capability =
itself. It needs to be used in conjunction with a scaling solution like VMS=
S or Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS).</p>
<p><b>D. Public load balancer</b> - A public load balancer is used to distr=
ibute traffic from the internet to VMs within a virtual network. Like the i=
nternal load balancer, it doesn't provide scaling capabilities on its own. =
It's a traffic distribution mechanism, not a scaling solution.</p>
