<p><b>Correct answer: Compute cost</b></p>
<p>Azure Migrate assesses the on-premises virtual machines and provides a c=
ost estimation for running those machines in Azure. The compute cost is a s=
ignificant part of the overall cost, and Azure Migrate calculates this base=
d on the VM size, region, and operating system. It considers factors like C=
PU, memory, and the chosen Azure VM instance type.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Correct answer: Storage cost</b></p>
<p>Azure Migrate also estimates the storage cost for migrating the on-premi=
ses virtual machines to Azure. This includes the cost of Azure storage acco=
unts, the type of storage (e.g., Standard HDD, Standard SSD, Premium SSD), =
and the amount of storage required based on the disks attached to the VMs. =
The tool considers factors like data redundancy and the chosen Azure storag=
e tier.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. Network cost</b> - While network costs are a factor in Azure, Azur=
e Migrate's primary focus is on the compute and storage aspects of the migr=
ation. It does not provide a detailed breakdown of all potential network co=
sts, such as inter-region traffic or VPN gateway costs. These costs are hig=
hly dependent on the specific network architecture and usage patterns, whic=
h are beyond the scope of the initial assessment.</p>
<p><b>B. Bandwith cost</b> - Bandwidth costs are associated with data trans=
fer in and out of Azure. While data egress costs are a factor, Azure Migrat=
e does not directly estimate bandwidth costs. The tool focuses on the cost =
of running the VMs and storing the data in Azure. Bandwidth costs are depen=
dent on the amount of data transferred, which is not something Azure Migrat=
e can accurately predict during the assessment phase.</p>
