 messaging. Given the requirement for reliability in a purchase order scena=
rio, Service Bus is the most appropriate choice. It offers features like gu=
aranteed message delivery (using queues and topics), transaction support, a=
nd dead-letter queues for handling failed messages. These features are cruc=
ial for ensuring that purchase orders are processed correctly and reliably,=
 even in the face of transient failures or system outages. Service Bus supp=
orts both queue and publish/subscribe models, making it flexible for differ=
ent messaging patterns.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. Event Hub</b> - Event Hubs is designed for high-throughput data st=
reaming and ingestion, primarily for telemetry and event data. While it can=
 handle a large volume of events, it doesn't offer the same level of guaran=
teed delivery and transactional support as Service Bus, which are critical =
for reliable purchase order processing.</p>
<p><b>B. Notification Hub</b> - Notification Hubs is a mobile push notifica=
tion engine. It's used to send notifications to mobile devices and is not s=
uitable for reliable messaging between systems for purchase orders.</p>
<p><b>C. Event Grid</b> - Event Grid is an event routing service that enabl=
es reactive programming. It's designed for event-driven architectures where=
 applications react to events in near real-time. While it provides reliable=
 delivery, it's best suited for event notifications and not for guaranteed =
message delivery and processing required for purchase orders. Event Grid is=
 push-based, whereas Service Bus is pull-based, which is often preferred fo=
r reliable message processing.</p>
