<p><b>Correct answer: Log: None Data: ReadOnly</b></p>
<p>This is the correct answer because it provides the optimal balance betwe=
en performance and data integrity for SQL Server workloads. SQL Server log =
files require a caching policy of <code>None</code> to ensure that all writ=
e operations are immediately persisted to disk. This prevents data loss in =
the event of a system failure. For the data disk, a <code>ReadOnly</code> c=
aching policy is appropriate. This allows the Azure infrastructure to cache=
 reads from the data disk, improving performance for read-intensive operati=
ons. Write operations will still be written directly to the disk, ensuring =
data integrity.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>B. Log: ReadOnly Data: ReadOnly</b> - Using <code>ReadOnly</code> cac=
hing for the log disk is incorrect. SQL Server log files are write-intensiv=
e, and the <code>ReadOnly</code> cache setting is optimized for read operat=
ions. More importantly, caching writes to the log files can lead to data lo=
ss or corruption if the cache is not properly flushed to disk before a fail=
ure. The SQL Server transaction log requires that writes are immediately pe=
rsisted to disk.</p>
<p><b>C. Log: ReadWrite Data: ReadWrite</b> - Using <code>ReadWrite</code> =
caching for either the log or data disks is generally not recommended for S=
QL Server workloads. While it can improve performance, it introduces a risk=
 of data loss or corruption if the cache is not properly flushed to disk be=
fore a failure. SQL Server relies on the integrity of the transaction log f=
or recovery, and <code>ReadWrite</code> caching can compromise this integri=
ty. Using <code>ReadWrite</code> caching requires careful consideration and=
 testing to ensure data integrity, and is typically not the default recomme=
ndation.</p>
