<p><b>Correct answer: Always Encrypted</b></p>
<p>Always Encrypted is the correct choice because it addresses both require=
ments: encrypting data in transit and at rest, and preventing database admi=
nistrators from viewing sensitive data. Always Encrypted allows client appl=
ications to encrypt sensitive data inside the client application before sto=
ring it in Azure SQL Database. This means the data is encrypted during tran=
sit and at rest. Database administrators, or other privileged users who do =
not have access to the encryption keys, cannot view the plaintext data. The=
 encryption keys reside within the client application's trusted environment=
, ensuring separation of duties.</p>
<br><br>
<p><b>Incorrect:</b></p>
<p><b>A. Transparent Data Encryption (TDE)</b> - TDE encrypts the entire da=
tabase at rest, meaning the data files themselves are encrypted. While TDE =
protects against offline attacks (e.g., someone stealing the physical datab=
ase files), it doesn't prevent database administrators from viewing the dat=
a within the database. The database server has access to the decryption key=
, so anyone with sufficient privileges on the database server can see the u=
nencrypted data. Therefore, TDE does not satisfy the requirement of prevent=
ing database administrators from viewing sensitive information.</p>
